D("object",{etymology:E("From Latin obiectum ‘something presented’, neuter of obiectus, from obicere ‘throw before’; it later named a thing, aim, grammatical object, and the verb ‘oppose’.",null,"Latin obiectum < obicere",null,"Etymonline, object (https://www.etymonline.com/word/object)."),contexts:[C("object.s1","通用","A small metal object lay beside the magnet on the table.","桌上的磁鐵旁放著一個小金屬物件。","Object means a physical thing that can be seen or touched.","Count noun modified by small and metal; lay is past tense of lie.","The metal item is tangible, selecting the concrete noun sense."),C("object.s2","书面","Her main object was to keep the community garden open through winter.","她的主要目標是讓社區花園整個冬天都保持開放。","Object means the aim someone is trying to achieve.","Possessive adjective + object; was to introduces the intended goal.","Keeping the garden open is a purpose, not a physical thing."),C("object.s3","语法","In “They invited her,” her is the object of the verb invited.","在“They invited her”中，her 是動詞 invited 的賓語。","Object is the person or thing receiving a verb’s action.","The quoted clause illustrates a direct object after a transitive verb.","Her receives the action of inviting, matching the grammar sense."),C("object.s4","通用","Several residents objected to the plan because it removed the only bus stop.","幾位居民反對這項計畫，因為它取消了唯一的公車站。","As a verb, object means state disagreement with a proposal.","Object to + noun phrase; because gives the reason.","Residents express disagreement with a proposed change.")],mistakes:[M("collocation","Several residents objected the plan at the meeting.","Several residents objected to the plan at the meeting.","The verb meaning ‘express disagreement’ takes the preposition to before its target.")],quiz:[Q("object.q1","object.s1","meaning","Which item is an object in the physical sense?",["a glass marble on the desk","a goal for next year","the word her after a verb"],"a glass marble on the desk","The marble is a tangible thing that can be seen and touched.","meaning"),Q("object.q2","object.s2","meaning","The object of the campaign was to reduce food waste. Here object means ___.",["aim","solid item","grammatical receiver"],"aim","The infinitive states the result the campaign seeks, so object means purpose.","meaning"),Q("object.q3","object.s3","grammar","In “Maya repaired it,” which word is the object?",["it","Maya","repaired"],"it","It receives the action of repaired and functions as the direct object.","grammar"),Q("object.q4","object.s4","collocation","Choose the correct sentence for disagreeing with a rule.",["I object to the rule.","I object the rule.","I object with the rule."],"I object to the rule.","The disagreement verb uses object to something.","collocation")]});
D("object to",{etymology:E("The phrasal verb combines object, from Latin obicere ‘throw against’, with to marking the target of disagreement.",null,"Latin obicere",null,"Etymonline, object (https://www.etymonline.com/word/object)."),contexts:[C("object to.s1","社交","I object to publishing the children’s names without their parents’ consent.","我反對未經家長同意就公布孩子們的姓名。","Object to means say that one disagrees with or dislikes an action.","Object to + gerund; without introduces the missing consent.","The speaker voices opposition to the proposed publication.")],mistakes:[M("grammar","The neighbors objected to build the tower beside the school.","The neighbors objected to building the tower beside the school.","In object to, to is a preposition, so a following verb takes the -ing form.")],quiz:[Q("object to.q1","object to.s1","meaning","If residents object to a late-night venue, they ___.",["say they oppose it","say they built it","say they have never heard of it"],"say they oppose it","Object to expresses disagreement or dislike.","meaning"),Q("object to.q2","object to.s1","grammar","Choose the correct continuation: “She objected to ____ the data.”",["sharing","share","shared"],"sharing","The to in this phrase is a preposition and is followed by a gerund.","grammar"),Q("object to.q3","object to.s1","context","Which sentence expresses an objection?",["We object to closing the clinic before a replacement opens.","We objected the clinic last year.","We built the clinic after it closed."],"We object to closing the clinic before a replacement opens.","The sentence directly states opposition to a proposed closure.","context"),Q("object to.q4","object to.s1","word-choice","The residents disliked the proposal and formally ____ it.",["objected to","objectified","obliged"],"objected to","Object to is the phrasal verb for expressing disagreement with a proposal.","word-choice")]});
D("objectify",{etymology:E("Formed from object + -ify ‘make or treat as’; the figurative verb means treat a person as a thing, while a separate use makes an abstract idea concrete.",null,"object","-ify","Etymonline, objectify (https://www.etymonline.com/word/objectify)."),contexts:[C("objectify.s1","社会","The campaign asks advertisers not to objectify women by reducing them to body parts.","這項倡議呼籲廣告商不要把女性簡化成身體部位、將她們物化。","Objectify means treat a person as an object without regard for their humanity.","Ask + object + infinitive; by + gerund states the means.","Reducing people to body parts ignores their personhood, matching the critical sense."),C("objectify.s2","学术","The diagram helped objectify the abstract model by showing its variables as measurable quantities.","這張圖把模型的變數呈現為可測量的量，幫助將抽象模型具體化。","Objectify can mean express an abstract idea in a concrete form.","Helped + bare infinitive; by introduces the method.","A diagram gives an abstract model a concrete, measurable representation.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The film objectified the actor by giving him a complex voice and independent choices.","The film avoided objectifying the actor by giving him a complex voice and independent choices.","Complex agency and independent choices resist reducing a person to a mere object.")],quiz:[Q("objectify.q1","objectify.s1","meaning","A report says a campaign objectifies workers. It criticizes the campaign for ___.",["treating people as things","giving workers a concrete chart","offering every worker a promotion"],"treating people as things","The social sense is to disregard a person’s humanity and treat them as an object.","meaning"),Q("objectify.q2","objectify.s2","meaning","A model is objectified when an abstract idea is ___.",["given a concrete form","kept entirely unexpressed","made morally blameworthy"],"given a concrete form","This less common sense is to express an abstraction in a tangible or clear representation.","meaning"),Q("objectify.q3","objectify.s1","context","Which scene shows objectifying treatment?",["An ad presents a person only as a body for others to buy products.","A portrait gives its subject a full biography.","A teacher asks a student for her own interpretation."],"An ad presents a person only as a body for others to buy products.","The ad reduces the person to an object rather than respecting her humanity.","context"),Q("objectify.q4","objectify.s2","word-formation","Complete: “The chart helps ____ the theory for new readers.”",["objectify","objection","objective"],"objectify","The verb objectify can mean make an abstract theory concrete or tangible.","word-formation")]});
D("objection",{etymology:E("From Old French objection and Latin obiectio, from obicere ‘throw against’; an objection is a point raised against a proposal.",null,"Latin obiectio < obicere",null,"Etymonline, objection (https://www.etymonline.com/word/objection)."),contexts:[C("objection.s1","社交","Her main objection to the schedule was that it left no time for lunch.","她對這份時間表的主要異議是，它沒有留出午餐時間。","Objection is a statement or feeling of disagreement.","Objection to + noun; that clause explains the reason.","She raises a specific criticism of the schedule.")],mistakes:[M("collocation","His objection against the plan was that it cost too much.","His objection to the plan was that it cost too much.","The usual preposition after objection is to when naming the proposal opposed.")],quiz:[Q("objection.q1","objection.s1","meaning","A formal objection to a plan is ___.",["a statement of disagreement","a detailed timetable","a final approval"],"a statement of disagreement","An objection expresses disagreement with something.","meaning"),Q("objection.q2","objection.s1","collocation","Choose the natural phrase.",["an objection to the proposal","an objection at the proposal","an objection for the proposal"],"an objection to the proposal","Objection is conventionally followed by to when identifying its target.","collocation"),Q("objection.q3","objection.s1","context","Which remark is an objection?",["I cannot support the plan because it blocks the only entrance.","The plan begins on Tuesday.","The plan has a blue cover."],"I cannot support the plan because it blocks the only entrance.","The speaker provides a reason for opposing the proposal.","context"),Q("objection.q4","objection.s1","word-choice","The committee heard one ____ before voting against the change.",["objection","objectionable","objectively"],"objection","The sentence needs a count noun naming a statement of disagreement.","word-choice")]});
D("objective",{etymology:E("From Medieval Latin obiectivus, from obiectum ‘thing presented’; the English noun ‘aim’ and adjective ‘fact-based’ developed as distinct uses.",null,"Medieval Latin obiectivus < obiectum",null,"Etymonline, objective (https://www.etymonline.com/word/objective)."),contexts:[C("objective.s1","通用","Their first objective is to restore the footbridge before the rainy season.","他們的首要目標是在雨季前修復人行橋。","Objective as a noun means a result a person or group works to achieve.","Possessive determiner + objective; is to introduces the goal.","Restoring the bridge is a planned result."),C("objective.s2","通用","The reviewer used objective criteria, including cost and completion time, rather than personal preference.","審查者使用客觀標準，包括成本和完工時間，而非個人偏好。","Objective as an adjective means based on facts rather than feelings or bias.","Attributive adjective before criteria; including gives examples.","Cost and time are measurable criteria, contrasted with preference."),C("objective.s3","语法","In “They called him,” him is the objective form of the pronoun.","在“They called him”中，him 是代詞的賓格形式。","Objective describes a pronoun form used as an object.","Predicate adjective after is; of introduces the pronoun category.","Him is the object form corresponding to subject-form he.")],mistakes:[M("confusable","The reviewer gave an objective opinion based only on her personal likes.","The reviewer gave a subjective opinion based on her personal likes.","An opinion based on personal preference is subjective, not objective.")],quiz:[Q("objective.q1","objective.s1","meaning","A project objective is the ___.",["result the team aims to achieve","person receiving a verb’s action","bias in an opinion"],"result the team aims to achieve","As a noun, objective means the goal being worked toward.","meaning"),Q("objective.q2","objective.s2","meaning","An objective report should rely mainly on ___.",["verifiable facts","the writer’s mood","a rumor"],"verifiable facts","The adjective means based on facts rather than personal feelings.","meaning"),Q("objective.q3","objective.s3","grammar","Which is the objective pronoun form in “The coach praised ___”?",["him","he","hisly"],"him","Him can function as the object of praised; he is the subject form.","grammar"),Q("objective.q4","objective.s2","context","Which review is most objective?",["It compares measured costs and dates against published criteria.","It rejects the result because the reviewer dislikes the color.","It repeats an unverified rumor."],"It compares measured costs and dates against published criteria.","Measured evidence and stated criteria minimize personal bias.","context")]});
D("objective case",{etymology:E("A grammar term combining objective with case, the grammatical form used for a noun or pronoun functioning as the object of a verb or preposition.",null,"objective + case",null,"Merriam-Webster, objective case (https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/objective%20case)."),contexts:[C("objective case.s1","语法","In “She sent him a map,” him is in the objective case after the verb sent.","在“She sent him a map”中，him 位於動詞 sent 後，使用賓格。","Objective case names the form used for an object of a verb or preposition.","Him follows the verb as an indirect object; a map is the direct object.","The pronoun form him, rather than he, receives the verb’s action.")],mistakes:[M("grammar","The sentence “They helped she” uses the objective case correctly.","The sentence “They helped her” uses the objective case correctly.","Her is the objective form; she is the subject form.")],quiz:[Q("objective case.q1","objective case.s1","meaning","The objective case marks a noun or pronoun used as ___.",["the object of a verb or preposition","the subject of every clause","a verb’s tense ending"],"the object of a verb or preposition","That is the defining grammatical function of the objective case.","meaning"),Q("objective case.q2","objective case.s1","grammar","Choose the objective pronoun: “The guide showed ___ the map.”",["us","we","ourselfs"],"us","Us is the object form after showed; we is the subject form.","grammar"),Q("objective case.q3","objective case.s1","context","Which sentence contains a pronoun in the objective case?",["The nurse called them.","They called the nurse.","Their call was brief."],"The nurse called them.","Them receives the action of called and is an objective form.","context"),Q("objective case.q4","objective case.s1","word-choice","After the preposition to, choose the objective form: “to ____.”",["her","she","herselfs"],"her","A pronoun after a preposition uses its objective form.","word-choice")]});
D("objectively",{etymology:E("Formed from objective + -ly; it means in a way based on observable facts rather than personal bias.",null,"objective","-ly","Etymonline, objective (https://www.etymonline.com/word/objective)."),contexts:[C("objectively.s1","通用","The panel assessed each bid objectively by applying the same published scoring rules.","評審小組依照相同的公開評分規則，客觀地評估每份投標書。","Objectively modifies assessed and describes a fact-based method.","Adverb after the verb and before the manner phrase; by introduces method.","The same published rules reduce personal preference in evaluation.")],mistakes:[M("word-formation","The panel judged the bids objective, using the same rules for each one.","The panel judged the bids objectively, using the same rules for each one.","The word modifies the action judged, so the adverb objectively is needed.")],quiz:[Q("objectively.q1","objectively.s1","meaning","To assess a bid objectively is to use ___.",["consistent evidence-based criteria","personal favoritism","an unexplained hunch"],"consistent evidence-based criteria","Objectively means according to observable facts rather than bias.","meaning"),Q("objectively.q2","objectively.s1","grammar","Choose the adverb: “The editor evaluated the claim ____.”",["objectively","objective","objectivity"],"objectively","The blank modifies evaluated, so an adverb is required.","grammar"),Q("objectively.q3","objectively.s1","context","Which action is most objective?",["Two reviewers score every application against the same rubric.","One reviewer rewards a friend without reading the form.","A reviewer rejects a file because of its color."],"Two reviewers score every application against the same rubric.","A common rubric applies the same criteria across cases.","context"),Q("objectively.q4","objectively.s1","word-choice","The lab recorded the temperature ____ instead of estimating it by feel.",["objectively","objective","objectivity"],"objectively","The sentence needs an adverb describing how the lab recorded the value.","word-choice")]});
D("objectivity",{etymology:E("From objective + -ity, the suffix forming an abstract noun for a quality or state; object is from Latin obiectum.",null,"objective < Latin obiectum","-ity","Etymonline, objective (https://www.etymonline.com/word/objective)."),contexts:[C("objectivity.s1","学术","The researchers protected objectivity by preregistering how they would score each response.","研究人員預先登記每份回答的評分方式，以維持客觀性。","Objectivity is judgment grounded in facts rather than bias.","Uncountable abstract noun as the object of protected; by introduces means.","The scoring plan is fixed before seeing results, limiting subjective changes.")],mistakes:[M("word-formation","The editor praised the report for being objective and its objectivityness.","The editor praised the report for being objective and its objectivity.","Objectivity is the established abstract noun; objectivityness adds an unnecessary suffix.")],quiz:[Q("objectivity.q1","objectivity.s1","meaning","A study’s objectivity is its freedom from ___.",["personal bias","measurable evidence","clear methods"],"personal bias","Objectivity means making judgments from facts rather than bias.","meaning"),Q("objectivity.q2","objectivity.s1","word-formation","Choose the abstract noun: “The committee valued the ____ of the review.”",["objectivity","objective","objectively"],"objectivity","The noun after the requires the quality noun objectivity.","word-formation"),Q("objectivity.q3","objectivity.s1","context","Which practice best supports objectivity?",["Use criteria fixed before reviewing results.","Change scores to favor a friend.","Ignore evidence that challenges a preferred answer."],"Use criteria fixed before reviewing results.","Predefined standards reduce bias in evaluating evidence.","context"),Q("objectivity.q4","objectivity.s1","meaning","Why might a reader question a report’s objectivity?",["It relies on the author’s preference instead of evidence.","It records dates and measurements.","It explains how results were scored."],"It relies on the author’s preference instead of evidence.","Dependence on preference undermines evidence-based judgment.","meaning")]});
D("oblation",{etymology:E("From Old French oblation and Latin oblatio ‘offering’, from oblatus, the past participle of offerre ‘offer’; in religious use it is a ceremonial gift to God.",null,"Latin oblatio < oblatus",null,"Etymonline, oblation (https://www.etymonline.com/word/oblation)."),contexts:[C("oblation.s1","宗教","The congregation placed bread and wine on the altar as an oblation during the service.","禮拜期間，會眾把麵包和葡萄酒放在祭壇上作為奉獻。","Oblation is a gift offered to God, especially in a ceremony.","As an oblation is a role phrase; during situates the offering in a service.","The altar and ritual offering make the religious sense explicit.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The congregation called the parking ticket an oblation to the city during worship.","The congregation offered bread as an oblation during worship.","An oblation is a ceremonial offering to a deity, not an ordinary fee or ticket.")],quiz:[Q("oblation.q1","oblation.s1","meaning","An oblation in a religious service is ___.",["a ceremonial offering","a court objection","a wooden rowing tool"],"a ceremonial offering","The word names a gift offered to God, especially in a ritual.","meaning"),Q("oblation.q2","oblation.s1","context","Which item could be presented as an oblation?",["bread placed on an altar during worship","a bus ticket used for commuting","a key left at a hotel desk"],"bread placed on an altar during worship","The ritual setting and altar identify a religious offering.","context"),Q("oblation.q3","oblation.s1","word-choice","The priest received the ceremonial ____ at the altar.",["oblation","oblivion","objection"],"oblation","Oblation means an offering made in a religious ceremony.","word-choice"),Q("oblation.q4","oblation.s1","meaning","Which origin best matches oblation?",["Latin for an offering","Old English for a tree","Greek for a telescope"],"Latin for an offering","The word descends from Latin oblatio, ‘offering’.","meaning")]});
D("obligate",{etymology:E("From Latin obligare ‘bind, oblige’, from ob- ‘to’ + ligare ‘bind’; the verb imposes a duty, while biology uses obligate for dependence on one condition.","ob-","ligare","-ate","Etymonline, obligate (https://www.etymonline.com/word/obligate)."),contexts:[C("obligate.s1","法律","The lease obligates the landlord to repair the heating system within ten days.","租約要求房東在十天內修理暖氣系統。","Obligate means legally or morally require someone to act.","Obligate + person + to-infinitive; within sets the deadline.","A lease creates a binding duty to repair."),C("obligate.s2","生物","An obligate parasite can survive only inside a host organism.","專性寄生生物只能在宿主體內存活。","In biology, obligate describes an organism restricted to a particular condition.","Attributive adjective before parasite; only emphasizes the restriction.","Survival depends exclusively on a host, matching the biological sense.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","A facultative organism that can live with or without a host is an obligate parasite.","An organism that can live with or without a host is not an obligate parasite.","Obligate indicates dependence on a required condition; facultative describes flexibility.")],quiz:[Q("obligate.q1","obligate.s1","meaning","A contract that obligates a seller to deliver goods ___.",["makes delivery a duty","suggests delivery as an optional favor","cancels the sale"],"makes delivery a duty","The verb creates a legal or promised requirement.","meaning"),Q("obligate.q2","obligate.s2","meaning","An obligate parasite must ___.",["depend on a host to survive","survive equally well without a host","avoid living organisms"],"depend on a host to survive","The biological adjective means restricted to a necessary condition such as a host.","meaning"),Q("obligate.q3","obligate.s1","grammar","Complete the pattern: “The order obligates us ____ the records.”",["to preserve","preserving","preserve to"],"to preserve","Obligate + object is followed by a to-infinitive.","grammar"),Q("obligate.q4","obligate.s2","context","Which description fits an obligate anaerobe?",["It can grow only without oxygen.","It grows only in bright sunlight.","It can grow equally with or without oxygen."],"It can grow only without oxygen.","Obligate signals that the organism requires the specified condition.","context")]});
D("obligated",{etymology:E("Past participle and adjective from obligate, from Latin obligare ‘bind’; obligated describes a duty or a felt debt after receiving a favor.",null,"Latin obligare","-ed","Etymonline, obligated (https://www.etymonline.com/word/obligated)."),contexts:[C("obligated.s1","通用","After her colleague covered the shift, Mina felt obligated to return the favor.","同事替米娜代班後，她覺得自己有責任回報這份人情。","Obligated means feeling required by a duty or favor.","Felt + adjective complement; obligated to + infinitive states the response.","A colleague’s favor creates a sense of indebted duty.")],mistakes:[M("grammar","Mina felt obligated returning the favor after her colleague helped.","Mina felt obligated to return the favor after her colleague helped.","The usual construction is obligated to + base verb.")],quiz:[Q("obligated.q1","obligated.s1","meaning","If you feel obligated to repay a favor, you feel ___.",["you ought to do it","you must forget it","you are unable to understand it"],"you ought to do it","Obligated describes a perceived duty caused by a responsibility or favor.","meaning"),Q("obligated.q2","obligated.s1","grammar","Choose the standard form: “I felt obligated ____.”",["to respond","responding","responded"],"to respond","Obligated is followed by a to-infinitive in this construction.","grammar"),Q("obligated.q3","obligated.s1","context","Which situation most naturally makes someone feel obligated?",["A neighbor watered your plants while you were away.","A stranger walked past your house.","A clock showed the time."],"A neighbor watered your plants while you were away.","Receiving meaningful help may create a felt duty to return the kindness.","context"),Q("obligated.q4","obligated.s1","word-choice","Because the agreement requires it, the supplier is ____ to replace the faulty part.",["obligated","obligation","obligatingly"],"obligated","The sentence needs an adjective describing the supplier’s required duty.","word-choice")]});
D("obligation",{etymology:E("From Old French obligation and Latin obligatio ‘a binding’; from obligare, ob- ‘to’ + ligare ‘bind’.",null,"Latin obligatio < obligare",null,"Etymonline, obligation (https://www.etymonline.com/word/obligation)."),contexts:[C("obligation.s1","社会","The tenants have an obligation to report water leaks before they damage the building.","租戶有義務在漏水損壞建築物前通報。","Obligation is a moral or legal duty to do something.","Have an obligation to + infinitive; before marks the time limit.","The duty arises from tenancy responsibilities and prevents harm.")],mistakes:[M("collocation","The tenants have an obligation for report the leak promptly.","The tenants have an obligation to report the leak promptly.","Obligation is followed by to + base verb when naming the required action.")],quiz:[Q("obligation.q1","obligation.s1","meaning","A legal obligation is ___.",["a duty required by law","a hobby someone may ignore","a casual prediction"],"a duty required by law","Obligation names a legal or moral duty.","meaning"),Q("obligation.q2","obligation.s1","grammar","Complete: “We have an obligation ____ the borrowed tools.”",["to return","returning","for return"],"to return","The noun obligation is followed by to and a base verb.","grammar"),Q("obligation.q3","obligation.s1","context","Which is an obligation under a rental agreement?",["Paying rent by the agreed date","Choosing a favorite color","Watching a film on Friday"],"Paying rent by the agreed date","The contract creates a duty to pay rent on time.","context"),Q("obligation.q4","obligation.s1","word-choice","A duty owed to another person can be called an ____. ",["obligation","obligate","obligingly"],"obligation","Obligation is the noun naming the duty itself.","word-choice")]});
D("obligatory",{etymology:E("From Medieval Latin obligatorius, from obligare ‘bind’; the adjective describes what a law, rule, or custom requires.",null,"Latin obligatorius < obligare",null,"Etymonline, obligatory (https://www.etymonline.com/word/obligatory)."),contexts:[C("obligatory.s1","社会","Safety goggles are obligatory in the workshop whenever the cutting tools are running.","只要切割工具正在運轉，工作間就必須佩戴護目鏡。","Obligatory means required by a rule or custom.","Predicate adjective after are; whenever states the condition.","The workshop rule makes eye protection mandatory.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The lab made protective goggles obligatory but said workers could ignore the rule.","The lab made protective goggles obligatory and required workers to follow the rule.","Obligatory means mandatory, so saying the rule may be ignored contradicts it.")],quiz:[Q("obligatory.q1","obligatory.s1","meaning","If a form is obligatory, completing it is ___.",["required","purely decorative","forbidden"],"required","Obligatory means required by a rule, law, or custom.","meaning"),Q("obligatory.q2","obligatory.s1","context","Which item is obligatory under the workshop rule?",["Wearing eye protection near active cutting tools","Choosing a blue notebook","Arriving whenever one likes"],"Wearing eye protection near active cutting tools","The stated safety rule makes goggles mandatory in that condition.","context"),Q("obligatory.q3","obligatory.s1","word-formation","Choose the adjective: “Attendance is ____ for all trainees.”",["obligatory","obligation","obligatorily"],"obligatory","The blank after is needs an adjective describing attendance.","word-formation"),Q("obligatory.q4","obligatory.s1","meaning","An obligatory signature is one that ___.",["must be provided","may be omitted without consequence","is a personal hobby"],"must be provided","A required signature is mandatory rather than optional.","meaning")]});
D("oblige",{etymology:E("From Old French obliger and Latin obligare ‘bind’, ob- ‘to’ + ligare ‘bind’; meanings include impose a duty, do a favor, and make someone grateful.","ob-","ligare","-e","Etymonline, oblige (https://www.etymonline.com/word/oblige)."),contexts:[C("oblige.s1","书面","The regulation obliges the company to publish its safety results each year.","規定要求公司每年公布安全結果。","Oblige means require someone to act because of law, duty, or circumstance.","Oblige + object + to-infinitive; each year gives frequency.","A regulation imposes a reporting duty."),C("oblige.s2","口语","Could you oblige me by holding the door while I carry these boxes?","我搬這些箱子時，你能幫忙扶一下門嗎？","Oblige means do someone a requested kindness.","Polite modal request; oblige + person + by + gerund names the favor.","Holding the door is a small requested service."),C("oblige.s3","书面","I am much obliged to the neighbors who watched our house during the storm.","我非常感激暴風雨期間替我們看家的鄰居。","Obliged can mean grateful for a kindness received.","Much obliged is a set expression; to introduces the people thanked.","The neighbors’ help prompts gratitude, not compulsion.")],mistakes:[M("grammar","The rule obliges workers wearing protective gloves.","The rule obliges workers to wear protective gloves.","When oblige means require a person to act, use object + to-infinitive.")],quiz:[Q("oblige.q1","oblige.s1","grammar","Complete: “The law obliges landlords ____ smoke alarms.”",["to install","installing","install"],"to install","Oblige + person is followed by a to-infinitive for a duty.","grammar"),Q("oblige.q2","oblige.s2","meaning","“Would you oblige me by carrying this box?” asks the listener to ___.",["do a requested favor","impose a legal penalty","express an objection"],"do a requested favor","This polite use means help by doing what someone asks.","meaning"),Q("oblige.q3","oblige.s3","meaning","If you are much obliged to a neighbor, you are ___.",["grateful","unaware","angry at a rule"],"grateful","The expression much obliged means very grateful.","meaning"),Q("oblige.q4","oblige.s2","context","Which sentence uses oblige as a favor?",["Please oblige us by moving your bicycle from the entrance.","The statute obliges firms to keep records.","I am obliged to the nurse for her help."],"Please oblige us by moving your bicycle from the entrance.","This is a courteous request for the listener to do something helpful.","context")]});
D("obliged",{etymology:E("Past participle of oblige, from Latin obligare ‘bind’; as an adjective it can mean required to act or, in the phrase much obliged, grateful.",null,"Latin obligare","-ed","Etymonline, obliged (https://www.etymonline.com/word/obliged)."),contexts:[C("obliged.s1","通用","Because the ferry was cancelled, we were obliged to take the longer road inland.","由於渡輪取消，我們只得走較長的內陸道路。","Obliged means forced or required by circumstances to do something.","Were + obliged to + infinitive; because gives the cause.","The cancellation leaves no practical alternative, so the travelers are required by circumstances.")],mistakes:[M("grammar","After the ferry stopped, we were obliged taking the inland road.","After the ferry stopped, we were obliged to take the inland road.","The adjective obliged is followed by a to-infinitive, not a gerund.")],quiz:[Q("obliged.q1","obliged.s1","meaning","If a storm leaves travelers obliged to stay, they ___.",["have to stay","are free to leave safely","have already forgotten the storm"],"have to stay","Obliged means required by a duty or situation.","meaning"),Q("obliged.q2","obliged.s1","grammar","Choose the standard construction.",["was obliged to wait","was obliged waiting","was obliged wait"],"was obliged to wait","Obliged is followed by to plus the base verb.","grammar"),Q("obliged.q3","obliged.s1","context","Which situation makes a crew obliged to turn back?",["A warning closes the only safe passage.","The crew prefers a different song.","The crew sees a pleasant harbor."],"A warning closes the only safe passage.","A safety closure creates a requirement imposed by circumstances.","context"),Q("obliged.q4","obliged.s1","word-choice","The road closure meant the driver was ____ to use the detour.",["obliged","obliging","obligation"],"obliged","The sentence needs the adjective meaning required by circumstances.","word-choice")]});
D("obliging",{etymology:E("From oblige + -ing; the adjective developed the sense ‘ready to do a kindness or comply with a request’.",null,"oblige","-ing","Etymonline, oblige (https://www.etymonline.com/word/oblige)."),contexts:[C("obliging.s1","通用","An obliging clerk carried the heavy parcel to the customer’s car.","一位熱心的店員把沉重包裹送到顧客的車旁。","Obliging describes someone ready to help in a friendly way.","Attributive adjective before clerk; carried records the helpful action.","The clerk willingly performs a useful service for the customer.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The obliging receptionist refused every request and would not answer a question.","The unhelpful receptionist refused every request and would not answer a question.","An obliging person is willing to help, so repeated refusal contradicts the adjective.")],quiz:[Q("obliging.q1","obliging.s1","meaning","An obliging neighbor is likely to ___.",["help carry a package when asked","hide the package","demand that nobody speak"],"help carry a package when asked","Obliging means friendly and ready to do what someone asks.","meaning"),Q("obliging.q2","obliging.s1","word-formation","Choose the adjective: “The ____ guide found a ramp for the visitor.”",["obliging","obligation","obligingly"],"obliging","The blank modifies guide, so the adjective is required.","word-formation"),Q("obliging.q3","obliging.s1","context","Which action is obliging?",["A librarian retrieves a book the visitor requested.","A clerk hides the requested form.","A driver refuses to stop at a safe crossing."],"A librarian retrieves a book the visitor requested.","The librarian willingly helps with the visitor’s request.","context"),Q("obliging.q4","obliging.s1","meaning","The phrase “an obliging host” suggests a host who is ___.",["helpful and accommodating","legally required to leave","unaware of every guest"],"helpful and accommodating","An obliging person responds helpfully to others’ requests.","meaning")]});
D("oblique",{etymology:E("From Latin obliquus ‘slanting, indirect’; it came through French oblique and retains both spatial and figurative senses.",null,"Latin obliquus",null,"Etymonline, oblique (https://www.etymonline.com/word/oblique)."),contexts:[C("oblique.s1","通用","Sunlight entered through an oblique window and formed a diagonal stripe on the floor.","陽光從一扇斜窗射入，在地板上形成一道對角光帶。","Oblique means slanting rather than vertical or horizontal.","Attributive adjective before window; and coordinates the resulting action.","The diagonal light indicates the window’s slanted orientation.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The arrow stood oblique at a perfect right angle to the floor.","The arrow stood upright at a perfect right angle to the floor.","Oblique means slanting; a perfect right angle to the floor describes upright, not oblique.")],quiz:[Q("oblique.q1","oblique.s1","meaning","An oblique line crosses a page ___.",["at a slant","in a perfect circle","only from top to bottom"],"at a slant","The spatial sense is diagonal or slanting rather than straight upright.","meaning"),Q("oblique.q2","oblique.s1","context","Which object is positioned obliquely?",["A ladder leaning against a wall","A post standing vertically","A shelf level with the floor"],"A ladder leaning against a wall","The ladder slants between the ground and wall.","context"),Q("oblique.q3","oblique.s1","word-choice","The beam struck the wall at an ____ angle.",["oblique","obligation","obligingly"],"oblique","The blank needs an adjective for a slanted angle.","word-choice"),Q("oblique.q4","oblique.s1","meaning","An oblique remark may also be ___.",["indirect rather than straightforward","a direct quotation","a precise measurement"],"indirect rather than straightforward","The word has a figurative sense for something not expressed directly.","meaning")]});
D("obliterate",{etymology:E("From Latin oblitterare ‘cause to be forgotten, erase’, from ob- + littera ‘letter’; the verb means erase or destroy completely.","ob-","littera","-ate","Etymonline, obliterate (https://www.etymonline.com/word/obliterate)."),contexts:[C("obliterate.s1","通用","The flood obliterated the footpath, leaving no clear trace of its route.","洪水徹底沖毀了小徑，沒有留下清晰的路線痕跡。","Obliterate means remove or destroy something completely.","Transitive verb with footpath as object; leaving introduces the result.","No clear trace remains, showing complete destruction rather than minor damage.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The light rain obliterated the stone bridge, leaving it unchanged and fully usable.","The flood obliterated the stone bridge, leaving only scattered stones.","Obliterate means destroy completely; an unchanged usable bridge contradicts that meaning.")],quiz:[Q("obliterate.q1","obliterate.s1","meaning","To obliterate a record is to ___.",["erase it completely","copy it neatly","read it aloud"],"erase it completely","Obliterate means remove or destroy completely.","meaning"),Q("obliterate.q2","obliterate.s1","context","Which event could obliterate a sand drawing?",["A strong wave washing it away","A label being added nearby","A visitor taking a photograph"],"A strong wave washing it away","The wave completely removes the drawing from the sand.","context"),Q("obliterate.q3","obliterate.s1","grammar","Choose the correct verb form: “The blast ____ the old markings.”",["obliterated","obliterating","obliteration"],"obliterated","The past-tense verb is needed to describe the completed destruction.","grammar"),Q("obliterate.q4","obliterate.s1","word-choice","The landslide completely erased the trail; it ____ the route.",["obliterated","obliged","objected to"],"obliterated","Obliterate is the verb for wiping out a route completely.","word-choice")]});
D("oblivion",{etymology:E("From Latin oblivio ‘forgetfulness’, from oblivisci ‘forget’; English uses include being forgotten by the public and a state of unawareness.",null,"Latin oblivio < oblivisci",null,"Etymonline, oblivion (https://www.etymonline.com/word/oblivion)."),contexts:[C("oblivion.s1","通用","After the final review, the once-famous slogan sank into oblivion and was no longer quoted.","最後一次評論之後，這句曾經有名的口號逐漸湮沒，再也沒有人引用。","Oblivion is the state of being forgotten by the public.","Sank into oblivion is an idiom; and coordinates the consequence.","No one quotes the slogan, so it has passed out of public memory."),C("oblivion.s2","通用","The exhausted climber slept in oblivion to the storm raging outside the shelter.","筋疲力盡的登山者熟睡著，對避難處外肆虐的暴風雨毫無察覺。","Oblivion can mean a state of being unaware of what is happening.","In oblivion to is followed by the event unnoticed; raging modifies storm.","The sleeper is unaware of the storm, selecting the unawareness sense.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The forgotten tune sank into oblivion, so everyone remembered and sang it daily.","The forgotten tune sank into oblivion, so almost nobody remembered it.","Sinking into oblivion means being forgotten, not being remembered by everyone.")],quiz:[Q("oblivion.q1","oblivion.s1","meaning","A custom that passes into oblivion is ___.",["forgotten by the public","made compulsory","translated into another language"],"forgotten by the public","This sense is the state of no longer being remembered.","meaning"),Q("oblivion.q2","oblivion.s2","meaning","Someone in oblivion to an alarm is ___.",["unaware that it is sounding","the person who set it","carefully repairing it"],"unaware that it is sounding","The second sense is lack of awareness of what is happening.","meaning"),Q("oblivion.q3","oblivion.s1","context","Which sentence uses oblivion as public forgetting?",["The old product name faded into oblivion.","She slept in oblivion to the thunder.","The alarm woke the entire house."],"The old product name faded into oblivion.","The product name is no longer remembered, matching the public-forgetting sense.","context"),Q("oblivion.q4","oblivion.s2","context","Which scene shows oblivion as unawareness?",["A patient sleeps through the noise, oblivious to it.","A song is forgotten for centuries.","An archive preserves every letter."],"A patient sleeps through the noise, oblivious to it.","The sleeper does not notice what is happening nearby.","context")]});
D("oblivious",{etymology:E("From Latin obliviosus ‘forgetful’, from oblivisci ‘forget’; the adjective now commonly means not noticing something present.",null,"Latin obliviosus < oblivisci",null,"Etymonline, oblivious (https://www.etymonline.com/word/oblivious)."),contexts:[C("oblivious.s1","通用","He was oblivious to the warning light until the machine began to beep.","直到機器開始鳴響，他才注意到警示燈。","Oblivious means not noticing something that is present.","Be oblivious to + noun; until marks when awareness changes.","The warning light was present but went unnoticed.")],mistakes:[M("collocation","She was oblivious of the flashing sign and walked into the closed area.","She was oblivious to the flashing sign and walked into the closed area.","The standard construction is oblivious to something.")],quiz:[Q("oblivious.q1","oblivious.s1","meaning","If a driver is oblivious to a warning sign, the driver ___.",["has not noticed it","has painted it","has followed it carefully"],"has not noticed it","Oblivious means unaware of something that is present.","meaning"),Q("oblivious.q2","oblivious.s1","collocation","Choose the standard phrase.",["oblivious to the noise","oblivious with the noise","oblivious at the noise"],"oblivious to the noise","The adjective is conventionally followed by to.","collocation"),Q("oblivious.q3","oblivious.s1","context","Which person is oblivious to the announcement?",["A listener wearing headphones who never hears it","A listener who repeats the announcement","A speaker who reads it aloud"],"A listener wearing headphones who never hears it","The announcement is present, but the listener does not notice it.","context"),Q("oblivious.q4","oblivious.s1","word-choice","The cyclist rode on, ____ to the loose strap dragging behind the bike.",["oblivious","oblivion","obligation"],"oblivious","An adjective is needed to describe the cyclist’s lack of awareness.","word-choice")]});
