D("obtainable",{etymology:E("Formed from obtain + -able; it describes something that can be acquired or secured.",null,"obtain","-able","Etymonline, obtain (https://www.etymonline.com/word/obtain)."),contexts:[C("obtainable.s1","通用","A printed copy of the old manual is still obtainable from the archive.","仍可從檔案館取得這本舊手冊的印刷本。","Obtainable means able to be obtained or acquired.","Predicate adjective after is; from identifies the source.","The archive still supplies a copy, so it can be acquired.")],mistakes:[M("word-formation","The rare handbook was obtain for a small fee from the archive.","The rare handbook was obtainable for a small fee from the archive.","The sentence needs the adjective meaning able to be obtained, not the base verb.")],quiz:[Q("obtainable.q1","obtainable.s1","meaning","If a replacement part is obtainable, it ___.",["can be acquired","cannot be found anywhere","has already broken"],"can be acquired","The suffix -able means capable of being obtained.","meaning"),Q("obtainable.q2","obtainable.s1","context","Which item is obtainable?",["A book the library can order for you","A document that was destroyed and cannot be copied","A key that no one has ever made"],"A book the library can order for you","The library can acquire and provide the book.","context"),Q("obtainable.q3","obtainable.s1","grammar","Choose the adjective: “The spare filter is ____ online.”",["obtainable","obtain","obtaining"],"obtainable","After is, the sentence needs an adjective describing availability.","grammar"),Q("obtainable.q4","obtainable.s1","word-formation","The suffix in obtainable means ___.",["capable of being obtained","the person who obtains","the past time of obtaining"],"capable of being obtained","-able forms an adjective meaning able to be acquired.","word-formation")]});
D("obtaining",{etymology:E("The -ing form of obtain, from Latin obtinere ‘get or acquire’; it names the act or process of getting something.",null,"Latin obtinere","-ing","Etymonline, obtain (https://www.etymonline.com/word/obtain)."),contexts:[C("obtaining.s1","通用","Obtaining written consent took longer than the team expected.","取得書面同意花的時間比團隊預期更久。","Obtaining is the process of getting or securing something.","Gerund phrase acts as the subject; took is its singular verb.","The team must secure consent, and the sentence describes that process.")],mistakes:[M("grammar","Obtaining the records were difficult because the office was closed.","Obtaining the records was difficult because the office was closed.","The gerund phrase is singular as a subject and takes was.")],quiz:[Q("obtaining.q1","obtaining.s1","meaning","Obtaining a license means ___.",["getting or securing it","losing it deliberately","printing it without approval"],"getting or securing it","Obtaining names the act of acquiring something.","meaning"),Q("obtaining.q2","obtaining.s1","context","Which activity is obtaining information?",["Requesting and receiving the archived report","Deleting the report","Ignoring the report"],"Requesting and receiving the archived report","The person actively gets the needed information.","context"),Q("obtaining.q3","obtaining.s1","grammar","Choose the correct verb: “Obtaining the permit ____ several forms.”",["requires","require","requiring"],"requires","The gerund phrase is singular and takes requires.","grammar"),Q("obtaining.q4","obtaining.s1","word-choice","The process of ____ consent began with a clear explanation.",["obtaining","obtainable","obligation"],"obtaining","The sentence needs the -ing noun form naming the process of getting consent.","word-choice")]});
D("obtrude",{etymology:E("From Latin obtrudere ‘thrust against or force upon’, ob- ‘against’ + trudere ‘push’; the verb means impose one’s presence or opinions intrusively.","ob-","trudere",null,"Etymonline, obtrude (https://www.etymonline.com/word/obtrude)."),contexts:[C("obtrude.s1","通用","He tried not to obtrude his advice while the family discussed the decision privately.","家人私下討論決定時，他盡量不把自己的建議強加給他們。","Obtrude means force one’s presence or opinions on others.","Try not to + verb; obtrude + object; while marks the ongoing discussion.","He restrains himself from imposing advice in a private conversation.")],mistakes:[M("grammar","She obtruded the group her opinion without waiting to be asked.","She obtruded her opinion on the group without waiting to be asked.","Obtrude takes the thing imposed as its object, followed by on + person; the original reverses these roles.")],quiz:[Q("obtrude.q1","obtrude.s1","meaning","To obtrude an opinion is to ___.",["force it on others intrusively","keep it private","ask permission before sharing it"],"force it on others intrusively","The verb means impose one’s presence or views without invitation.","meaning"),Q("obtrude.q2","obtrude.s1","collocation","Complete: “He kept trying to obtrude his advice ____ us.”",["on","at","from"],"on","The common construction is obtrude something on someone.","collocation"),Q("obtrude.q3","obtrude.s1","context","Which behavior is obtrusive?",["A guest repeatedly forces unwanted opinions into a private discussion.","A guest listens and waits to be invited.","A guest leaves quietly when asked."],"A guest repeatedly forces unwanted opinions into a private discussion.","The guest intrudes with views others did not request.","context"),Q("obtrude.q4","obtrude.s1","word-choice","The visitor tried not to ____ herself on the grieving family.",["obtrude","obtain","observe"],"obtrude","Obtrude fits the meaning of forcing one’s presence on others.","word-choice")]});
D("obviate",{etymology:E("From Latin obviare ‘meet or oppose’, from obvius ‘in the way’; English obviate means prevent a need or problem from arising.","ob-","via",null,"Etymonline, obviate (https://www.etymonline.com/word/obviate)."),contexts:[C("obviate.s1","通用","A shared online form will obviate the need to mail duplicate paper copies.","共用的線上表格將免去郵寄重複紙本的需要。","Obviate means remove or prevent the need for something.","Will + verb; obviate + the need; to mail specifies the action avoided.","The online form makes duplicate mailed copies unnecessary.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The backup plan obviated the delay, so the delay became unavoidable and still occurred.","The backup plan obviated the delay, so the delay did not occur.","Obviate means prevent a problem or need from arising; the original result contradicts it.")],quiz:[Q("obviate.q1","obviate.s1","meaning","A clear label can obviate confusion by ___.",["preventing the confusion from arising","making the confusion worse","hiding the label"],"preventing the confusion from arising","Obviate means eliminate or prevent a problem or need.","meaning"),Q("obviate.q2","obviate.s1","context","Which measure could obviate the need for a second trip?",["Bring every required document the first time.","Forget the appointment address.","Leave the form at home."],"Bring every required document the first time.","Having all documents avoids the need to return.","context"),Q("obviate.q3","obviate.s1","grammar","Choose the correct object: “The new route obviates ____.”",["the need for a ferry","to the ferry need","ferrying needly"],"the need for a ferry","Obviate takes a noun phrase such as the need for something.","grammar"),Q("obviate.q4","obviate.s1","meaning","If a repair obviates replacement, replacement is ___.",["no longer necessary","made compulsory","already completed"],"no longer necessary","Obviating something removes the need for it.","meaning")]});
D("obvious",{etymology:E("From Latin obvius ‘in the way, readily accessible’, ob- ‘toward’ + via ‘way’; English senses include easy to see and predictable or unimaginative.","ob-","via",null,"Etymonline, obvious (https://www.etymonline.com/word/obvious)."),contexts:[C("obvious.s1","通用","It was obvious from the muddy footprints that someone had entered the room.","從泥濘的腳印可以明顯看出有人進過房間。","Obvious means easy to notice or understand.","Predicate adjective after was; from introduces the evidence.","The visible footprints make the conclusion clear."),C("obvious.s2","通用","The sequel used an obvious twist that viewers had guessed in the opening scene.","續集採用了觀眾在開場就猜到的老套轉折。","Obvious can mean predictable and unimaginative.","Attributive adjective before twist; that clause explains predictability.","Viewers guessed it immediately, so the plot device lacks originality.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The answer was obvious, yet nobody could see or understand it even after it was explained.","The answer was obscure, since nobody could see or understand it even after it was explained.","Obvious means readily noticed or understood; the described difficulty fits obscure.")],quiz:[Q("obvious.q1","obvious.s1","meaning","An obvious error is one that is ___.",["easy to notice","impossible to detect","required by law"],"easy to notice","The first sense means readily seen or understood.","meaning"),Q("obvious.q2","obvious.s2","meaning","An obvious plot twist may feel ___.",["predictable and unoriginal","impossible to anticipate","scientifically measured"],"predictable and unoriginal","This sense describes something expected and unimaginative.","meaning"),Q("obvious.q3","obvious.s1","context","Which clue makes the answer obvious?",["A large arrow points directly to the marked door.","All labels have been removed.","The map is hidden in a locked room."],"A large arrow points directly to the marked door.","The arrow makes the intended choice easy to identify.","context"),Q("obvious.q4","obvious.s2","context","Which story choice is an obvious twist?",["The supposed stranger is revealed as the character’s twin, a guess made at the start.","The ending introduces a carefully foreshadowed surprise.","The narrator’s identity is never revealed."],"The supposed stranger is revealed as the character’s twin, a guess made at the start.","The audience anticipated the reveal early, so it is predictable.","context")]});
D("obviously",{etymology:E("Formed from obvious + -ly; it means in a way that is easy to see or understand, and can also mark an inference as self-evident.",null,"obvious","-ly","Etymonline, obvious (https://www.etymonline.com/word/obvious)."),contexts:[C("obviously.s1","通用","The path was obviously flooded because water covered every stepping stone.","路徑顯然淹水了，因為每塊踏石都被水覆蓋。","Obviously means in a way that is easy to see or understand.","Adverb modifies flooded; because gives the visible evidence.","Water over every stepping stone makes the condition unmistakable.")],mistakes:[M("word-formation","The path was obvious flooded after the storm.","The path was obviously flooded after the storm.","The adverb obviously modifies flooded; obvious is an adjective and cannot modify the verb phrase here.")],quiz:[Q("obviously.q1","obviously.s1","meaning","If a door is obviously open, it is ___.",["easy to see that it is open","hidden behind a wall","possibly locked but unseen"],"easy to see that it is open","Obviously marks something readily apparent.","meaning"),Q("obviously.q2","obviously.s1","grammar","Choose the adverb: “The answer is ____ incorrect.”",["obviously","obvious","obviousness"],"obviously","The adverb modifies the adjective incorrect.","grammar"),Q("obviously.q3","obviously.s1","context","Which evidence makes the leak obviously serious?",["Water is pouring through the ceiling onto electrical equipment.","A dry pipe is inspected once.","A sign is printed in blue."],"Water is pouring through the ceiling onto electrical equipment.","The visible water and electrical hazard make the seriousness clear.","context"),Q("obviously.q4","obviously.s1","word-choice","The sign was ____ damaged; half its letters had fallen off.",["obviously","obvious","obviousness"],"obviously","The sentence needs an adverb to modify damaged.","word-choice")]});
D("occasion",{etymology:E("From Old French occasion and Latin occasio ‘opportunity, fitting time’, from occasus/occidere ‘fall, set’; the noun names an event, a reason, or an opportunity, and the verb can cause something.",null,"Latin occasio < occidere",null,"Etymonline, occasion (https://www.etymonline.com/word/occasion)."),contexts:[C("occasion.s1","通用","On one occasion, the ferry ran after midnight because the harbor was closed by fog.","有一次，因港口起霧關閉，渡輪在午夜後仍有班次。","Occasion means a particular time when something happens.","On one occasion is an adverbial phrase; because gives the reason.","It refers to one specific instance."),C("occasion.s2","社交","Their wedding was a joyful occasion attended by friends from both families.","他們的婚禮是個歡樂的場合，兩家親友都出席了。","Occasion means a special event or ceremony.","Predicate noun after was; attended by is a reduced passive clause.","A wedding is a notable social event."),C("occasion.s3","通用","The broken seal gave the inspector occasion to examine the shipment more closely.","封條破損使檢查員有理由更仔細地檢查貨物。","Occasion means a reason or need for doing something.","Give someone occasion to + infinitive; the broken seal is the trigger.","The damage provides a reason for further inspection."),C("occasion.s4","通用","The sudden alarm occasioned a quick evacuation of the building.","突如其來的警報引發了大樓迅速疏散。","As a verb, occasion means cause something to happen.","Transitive past-tense verb; evacuation is its object.","The alarm causes the evacuation.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The alarm occasioned an evacuation, but no one left the building.","The alarm occasioned an evacuation, and everyone left the building.","To occasion an event is to cause it, so denying that it happened contradicts the verb.")],quiz:[Q("occasion.q1","occasion.s1","meaning","“On several occasions” means ___.",["at several times","at one special ceremony only","for no reason"],"at several times","Occasion can name a particular instance or time.","meaning"),Q("occasion.q2","occasion.s2","meaning","A graduation is an occasion because it is ___.",["a special event","a hidden obstacle","a grammatical object"],"a special event","The event sense applies to ceremonies and notable events.","meaning"),Q("occasion.q3","occasion.s3","meaning","If a delay gives occasion for concern, it provides ___.",["a reason to be concerned","a celebration","proof that no problem exists"],"a reason to be concerned","Occasion can mean a reason or cause for an action or feeling.","meaning"),Q("occasion.q4","occasion.s4","grammar","Choose the sentence where occasion is a verb meaning cause.",["The outage occasioned a review of the backup system.","We met on that occasion.","It was a festive occasion."],"The outage occasioned a review of the backup system.","Occasioned is a transitive verb with review as the result it caused.","grammar")]});
D("occasional",{etymology:E("From occasion + -al, ultimately Latin occasio; the adjective describes something happening sometimes but not often.",null,"Latin occasio","-al","Etymonline, occasional (https://www.etymonline.com/word/occasional)."),contexts:[C("occasional.s1","通用","An occasional shower cooled the garden during an otherwise dry week.","在其他日子都乾燥的一週裡，偶爾一場陣雨讓花園涼爽下來。","Occasional means happening from time to time but not frequently.","Attributive adjective before shower; otherwise contrasts the usual conditions.","The shower happens sometimes within a mostly dry period.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The occasional bus arrived every five minutes without interruption all day.","The frequent bus arrived every five minutes without interruption all day.","A service that runs every five minutes is frequent, not occasional.")],quiz:[Q("occasional.q1","occasional.s1","meaning","An occasional visitor comes ___.",["sometimes, but not often","every minute without a break","only once in history"],"sometimes, but not often","Occasional describes something that happens from time to time.","meaning"),Q("occasional.q2","occasional.s1","context","Which is an occasional event?",["A rare light frost appears a few times each winter.","The bell rings every hour all day.","The machine runs continuously."],"A rare light frost appears a few times each winter.","It happens sometimes but not regularly or frequently.","context"),Q("occasional.q3","occasional.s1","grammar","Choose the adjective: “We heard an ____ crack of thunder.”",["occasional","occasionally","occasion"],"occasional","The blank before crack needs an adjective.","grammar"),Q("occasional.q4","occasional.s1","word-choice","He makes an ____ visit to the village, usually once or twice a year.",["occasional","occasion","occasionally"],"occasional","The adjective modifies visit and signals infrequency.","word-choice")]});
D("occasionally",{etymology:E("Adverb formed from occasional + -ly, from occasion; it means sometimes, but not often.",null,"occasion","-ally","Etymonline, occasional (https://www.etymonline.com/word/occasional)."),contexts:[C("occasionally.s1","通用","The ferry occasionally stops at the smaller island when demand is high.","需求量高時，渡輪偶爾會停靠較小的島。","Occasionally means sometimes but not frequently.","Adverb modifies stops; when introduces the condition.","The stop occurs only on some trips, not on the regular schedule every time.")],mistakes:[M("word-formation","The ferry makes an occasionally stop at the island.","The ferry makes an occasional stop at the island.","The adjective occasional modifies the noun stop; occasionally is an adverb and cannot do so here.")],quiz:[Q("occasionally.q1","occasionally.s1","meaning","If a shop occasionally opens late, it does so ___.",["sometimes, but not often","every day without exception","never"],"sometimes, but not often","Occasionally means on some occasions, not frequently.","meaning"),Q("occasionally.q2","occasionally.s1","grammar","Choose the adverb: “The train ____ runs on a holiday schedule.”",["occasionally","occasional","occasion"],"occasionally","The blank modifies runs, so it needs an adverb.","grammar"),Q("occasionally.q3","occasionally.s1","context","Which pattern is occasional?",["A rare extra bus runs during a festival.","A bus departs every two minutes all year.","No bus ever runs."],"A rare extra bus runs during a festival.","The extra service happens only at some times.","context"),Q("occasionally.q4","occasionally.s1","word-choice","We ____ find fox tracks near the garden, but not every week.",["occasionally","occasional","occasion"],"occasionally","The sentence needs an adverb modifying find.","word-choice")]});
